r18-mutations run 1 (session 20, 2026-10-13T19:27:45Z to 21:02:53Z; the script it ran kept as
r18-mutations-v6-run1.sh, receipts r18-mutations-run1-shard{0,1,2}.{log,rc,start,end}; shard 0 exit 0, shards 1 and
2 rc 1), on the tree with tgc-2 applied: every one of the 303 rows ran exactly once and behaved as recorded, every
restore byte-identical and all eight baselines passing, except three carried v5 rows of the settled test, whose
behaviour T030o53's reading of brackets up to each reading's first word changed. D04 (vf_settled's open-code-span
condition) survived its row: the image bracket that row leaves open lies before the first word, so the head bracket
test keeps that candidate already. It is not equivalent: a code span left open across lines after an entity, holding
an image, puts no bracket before any reading's first word, so the open span alone keeps the candidate, and once the
span closes the inert reading's first word is the tier (r18-rows-probe-3.{cases,mut.nul,log,rc},
r18-rows-render-3.{log,rc}); that row was added in both passes as r18-edits/tgc-3.pairs (build-fixtures-3.py),
judged in a standalone copy (r18-fixtures-judge-3.{sh,log,rc,start,end}, exit 0: the repaired kernel passes both
changed functions, and D04 fails on both new rows), and applied after a hash check (r18-apply-3.{sh,log,rc}, exit 0,
r18-edits/post-apply-3.sha256; delta r18-session-3.diff, whole delta r18-session.diff). D02 (a settled candidate
never dropped), argued equivalent in v5 because only the cost changes, is a kill now: the 400-line timing row
T030o53 added bounds that cost, and its judge had run for over ten minutes when the invoker stopped the one awk
parsing that row at 20:30:03Z (the judge then failed with the row taking 624 s, not under 60 s, and the shard went
on); without a bound that run would take hours, so v6 runs each judge in its own process group under a 900 s
watchdog (pattern: t030o39-o40-mutations.sh) that stops it whole and records the diagnostic the D02 row now names.
D11 (the escape step of vf_settled_brackets) survived its row too, and is an argued equivalent now: the rendered
readings take escaped brackets out before they pair brackets, so a bracket that only an escaped one would close
stays open before the first word there; an escaped bracket before the first word is refused under it as well
(r18-rows-probe-4.{cases,mut.nul,log,rc}). All three are corrected in r18-mutations-gen.py; the run of record
re-runs every row on the tree with tgc-3 applied.
